# Git & GitHub Commands

This repository contains the Git and GitHub commands I learned while studying Git for DevOps.

---

# What is Git?

Git is a **Distributed Version Control System (DVCS)** that tracks changes in source code. It allows multiple developers to collaborate on the same project while maintaining a complete history of changes.

### Benefits of Git

* Tracks file changes
* Maintains project history
* Supports collaboration
* Enables branching and merging
* Allows reverting to previous versions
* Integrates with GitHub, GitLab, and Bitbucket

---

# Git Configuration

## Set Username

```bash
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
```

Sets your Git username globally.

---

## Set Email

```bash
git config --global user.email "your@email.com"
```

Sets your Git email globally.

---

## View Configuration

```bash
git config --list
```

Displays all configured Git settings.

---

# Repository Initialization

## Initialize Repository

```bash
git init
```

Creates a new Git repository in the current directory.

---

# Checking Repository Status

## Check Status

```bash
git status
```

Shows modified, staged, and untracked files.

---

# Staging Files

## Stage One File

```bash
git add filename
```

Stages a specific file.

---

## Stage All Files

```bash
git add .
```

Stages all modified files.

---

# Commit Changes

```bash
git commit -m "Your commit message"
```

Creates a snapshot of the staged changes.

---

# Branch Management

## List Branches

```bash
git branch
```

Displays all local branches.

---

## Rename Current Branch

```bash
git branch -m main
```

Renames the current branch to **main**.

---

## Switch Branch

```bash
git switch branch-name
```

Moves to another branch.

Example:

```bash
git switch feature-login
```

---

# Viewing History

## Show Commit History

```bash
git log
```

Displays the commit history.

---

## Detailed Commit History

```bash
git log -p -n 5
```

Shows the last 5 commits with code changes.

---

## Commit Statistics

```bash
git log --stat
```

Displays modified files and number of insertions/deletions.

---

## One-Line History

```bash
git log --pretty=oneline
```

Shows each commit in one line.

---

## Custom Log Format

```bash
git log --pretty=format:"%h - %an, %ar : %s"
```

Displays:

* Short Hash
* Author
* Relative Time
* Commit Message

---

## Search Commits by Function

```bash
git log -S "functionName"
```

Finds commits where a specific string was added or removed.

---

## Search Commit Message

```bash
git log --grep="login"
```

Finds commits containing a specific keyword.

---

## Commits Since a Date

```bash
git log --since="2026-08-01"
```

Shows commits after a specific date.

---

## Commits Until a Date

```bash
git log --until="2026-08-05"
```

Shows commits before a specific date.

---

## Commits by Author

```bash
git log --author="Ahmad"
```

Shows commits created by a specific author.

---

## Exclude Merge Commits

```bash
git log --no-merges
```

Shows only normal commits.

---

# Compare Changes

## View Differences

```bash
git diff
```

Shows changes that haven't been staged.

---

# View Commit Details

```bash
git show COMMIT_ID
```

Displays complete information about a commit.

---

## View a File from a Commit

```bash
git show COMMIT_ID:filename
```

Displays a file exactly as it existed in a specific commit.

---

# Restore Files

## Restore Working File

```bash
git restore filename
```

Discards local changes.

---

## Unstage File

```bash
git restore --staged filename
```

Removes a file from the staging area.

---

## Restore Working Tree

```bash
git restore --worktree filename
```

Restores only the working directory version.

---

## Restore File from a Commit

```bash
git checkout COMMIT_ID -- filename
```

Recovers a file from a previous commit.

---

## Switch Back to Main

```bash
git checkout main
```

Switches to the main branch.

---

# Reset Commits

## Soft Reset

```bash
git reset --soft HEAD^
```

Removes the last commit while keeping changes staged.

---

## Hard Reset

```bash
git reset --hard HEAD^
```

Deletes the last commit and all associated changes permanently.

---

# Remote Repositories

## View Remote Repository

```bash
git remote
```

Lists configured remotes.

---

## Push First Time

```bash
git push -u origin main
```

Pushes the current branch and sets the upstream branch.

---

# Clone Repository

```bash
git clone repository-url
```

Downloads an existing Git repository.

---

# Git Ignore

Create a file named:

```text
.gitignore
```

Example:

```text
node_modules/
.env
*.log
dist/
```

Git ignores these files and directories.

---

# Cleaning Files

## Preview Files to Remove

```bash
git clean -n
```

Shows which untracked files will be deleted.

---

## Delete Untracked Files

```bash
git clean -f
```

Removes untracked files.

---

## Delete Untracked Files and Directories

```bash
git clean -fd
```

Removes untracked files and folders.

---

# Tags

## List Tags

```bash
git tag
```

Displays all tags.

---

## Create Tag

```bash
git tag v1.0
```

Creates a lightweight tag.

---

## Push Tag

```bash
git push origin v1.0
```

Uploads the tag to GitHub.

---

# GitHub Basics

## Fork

Creates your own copy of another developer's repository on GitHub.

---

## Clone

Downloads a GitHub repository to your local machine.

---

## Push

Uploads local commits to GitHub.

---

## Pull *(Recommended to Learn)*

```bash
git pull
```

Downloads and merges changes from GitHub.

---

## Fetch *(Recommended to Learn)*

```bash
git fetch
```

Downloads remote changes without merging them.

---

## Merge *(Recommended to Learn)*

```bash
git merge branch-name
```

Merges another branch into the current branch.

---

## Stash *(Recommended to Learn)*

```bash
git stash
```

Temporarily saves uncommitted work.

Restore it:

```bash
git stash pop
```

---

## Reflog *(Recommended to Learn)*

```bash
git reflog
```

Shows every movement of HEAD and helps recover lost commits.

---

## Revert *(Recommended to Learn)*

```bash
git revert COMMIT_ID
```

Safely undoes a commit by creating a new commit.

---

## Blame *(Recommended to Learn)*

```bash
git blame filename
```

Shows who last modified each line of a file.

---

# Hosting a Static Website on GitHub

1. Push your project to GitHub.
2. Open the repository.
3. Go to **Settings**.
4. Click **Pages**.
5. Under **Source**, select:

   * Deploy from a branch
6. Select:

   * Branch: `main`
   * Folder: `/root`
7. Save the settings.
8. Wait a few minutes.
9. GitHub will generate a URL for your website.

Example:

```
https://username.github.io/repository-name/
```

